WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
Hi. This is Matt Baker. Today I’m going&nbsp;
to give you my answer to the question:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:09.040
What is the oldest European country? I say “my&nbsp;
answer” because, when it comes to this question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:15.120
the answer is not as clear cut as you might think.&nbsp;
For example, do we count microstates such as San&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:21.440
Marino or only regular sized ones like Italy?&nbsp;
Do we use the United Kingdom or do we consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.160
England, Scotland, and Wales separately? What&nbsp;
about countries that ceased to exist for some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.120
time, only to re-appear later, such as Po.&nbsp;
land? Do we count from their original founding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:53.360
date (966) or from their modern one (1918)?&nbsp;
As you can see, it's all very complicated.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:58.800
So, first of all, I want to make the point that&nbsp;
there were no European countries in ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:05.440
times. Sure, there were some very important&nbsp;
civilizations, such as the Greeks and Romans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:12.320
but in ancient times, they existed either in the&nbsp;
form of individual city-states or as empires.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:17.760
Countries (which are usually larger than a&nbsp;
city but smaller than an empire) came later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.960
sometime after the fall of Rome. They are&nbsp;
more regional in nature and in most cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:28.960
trace their origin to the Middle Ages, when some&nbsp;
local warlord united a bunch of nearby tribes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.600
usually based on a shared language and culture.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:37.360
It is for this reason that I chose to start&nbsp;
my Timeline of European History chart around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:43.440
the year 800 CE. Sure, there was obviously a&nbsp;
lot of history that occurred in Europe before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:48.720
this point but an argument could be made&nbsp;
that that was more Mediterranean history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:54.160
than European history, meaning that the center&nbsp;
of action was based around the Mediterranean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:02:00.560
Sea rather than on the continent of Europe as a&nbsp;
whole. You see, it wasn’t until the Middle Ages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:06.080
that the continent itself became the stage for&nbsp;
events because that’s when a bunch of countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:11.840
suddenly formed and started interacting with&nbsp;
each other as independent, regional entities.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:17.680
So, when it comes to figuring out which European&nbsp;
country is the oldest, I’m only going to be strict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:25.680
about two things. First, I’m not going include&nbsp;
any microstates. To me, they are anomalies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:32.160
don’t follow the usual pattern so I’m going&nbsp;
to focus on country-sized countries only. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:40.000
say goodbye to Andorra, Liechtenstein, Malta,&nbsp;
Monaco, San Marino, and Vatican City. Second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.720
when it comes to the transcontinental countries&nbsp;
(meaning those that can be considered both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:50.720
European and Asian), I am going to include Russia&nbsp;
but I’m not going to include any of the others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:57.360
such as Armenia, Azerbaijan, Cyprus, Georgia,&nbsp;
Kazakhstan, or Turkey (because, historically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:03.840
these countries are more closely linked to West&nbsp;
Asia than to Europe). However, when it comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:09.280
everything else, I’m going to be super lenient.&nbsp;
So, rather than focus on when each country got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:15.920
its current name, or achieved its modern borders&nbsp;
or in some cases, regained its independence, I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:23.520
instead just going to focus on when each country&nbsp;
first emerged as a unique self-governing entity.

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:27.760
Now, to make things easy, I’m next going&nbsp;
to remove any remaining countries that were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:34.320
formed in modern times (meaning those that were&nbsp;
never a unified, independent state during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:41.280
Middle Ages; those two words: unified and&nbsp;
independent being key factors). Obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.920
none of them are going to be among&nbsp;
the oldest—so this removes Austria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:54.800
Belgium, Estonia, Finland, Greece, Ireland,&nbsp;
Italy, Latvia, Luxembourg, Netherlands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:02.800
North Macedonia, Slovakia, and Slovenia. Which&nbsp;
means that we now have only 24 countries left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:08.080
(actually, 25, because I’m going to&nbsp;
consider England &amp; Scotland separately)

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:14.160
So, let's now go through each one, using this&nbsp;
Timeline of European History chart. We’ll start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:19.360
with the three main Scandinavian countries:&nbsp;
Denmark, Norway, and Sweden. As you can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:24.640
Norway was controlled by Sweden for most of&nbsp;
the 19th century and before that, by Denmark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:30.240
for around 300 years; and then before that: all&nbsp;
three were united as the Kalmar Union. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:34.960
during most of the Middle Ages, they were three&nbsp;
separate kingdoms. And, if we keep going up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:41.280
we discover that Norway is actually the oldest of&nbsp;
three, having been founded by Harald Fairhair in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:49.840
872. Denmark came next, in 936, and then Sweden in&nbsp;
970. Now, I do realize that these are traditional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:55.520
dates based on semi-legendary sources but&nbsp;
like I said, I’m going to be quite lenient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:00.880
(I’ll only come back to the issue of historicity&nbsp;
if we need to break a tie). Now, before we move on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:07.120
from the Nordic countries, I want to add Iceland.&nbsp;
Down here, I show that it became a part of Norway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:13.280
in the 13th century. However, before this, it&nbsp;
actually existed as an independent commonwealth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:19.280
starting from the year 930. So let’s&nbsp;
add Iceland to the list, above Denmark.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:26.160
Next up, let’s look at England and Scotland. They&nbsp;
merged to become Great Britain in 1707 (which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:31.520
today called the United Kingdom) but before&nbsp;
this, had very long histories of their own.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:38.720
As we move back up, I also want to address Wales&nbsp;
as we pass it. Like Ireland, Wales never really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:44.480
existed as a single, unified kingdom during&nbsp;
medieval times. The closest it came was when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:51.600
Llweylyn the Great established the Principality&nbsp;
of Wales but it was quickly absorbed into England.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:58.160
If we keep going up, we see that England was ruled&nbsp;
by Denmark for awhile but was founded in 886 by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:03.760
Alfred the Great (although it was actually his&nbsp;
grandson Aethelstan who first used the term “King&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:11.280
of the English”). Scotland is a little bit older,&nbsp;
having been founded by Kenneth MacAlpin in 843.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:17.200
Now, next, I want to move back down and&nbsp;
talk a bit about Spain. Like Great Britain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:22.800
Spain was actually formed by the merger of two&nbsp;
previously independent kingdoms, in this case:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:29.040
Castile and Aragon in 1479. Castile is&nbsp;
generally seen as being the dominant one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:34.480
so, since I’m being lenient, I’m not&nbsp;
going to use 1479 for Spain but instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:38.960
I’m going to continue to trace Castile&nbsp;
back a little further. As you can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:46.000
it too was actually formed by a merger between&nbsp;
two kingdoms, this time: Castile and Leon. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:52.400
if we keep going back, we see that, once upon a&nbsp;
time Spain actually consisted of four kingdoms:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:59.440
Castille, Leon, Aragon, and Navarre (with the&nbsp;
ruler of the largest one often using the title&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:07.120
“Emperor of All Spain”. So, I know it’s debatable,&nbsp;
but I’m going to use 1065 as the start date for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:12.880
Spain because that’s when there was, for the&nbsp;
first time ever, four Spanish kingdoms. Take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:20.480
note that Portugal actually formed a bit later,&nbsp;
in 1139, when it split off from Castile &amp; Leon.

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:27.440
Okay, we now come to a really big one: France.&nbsp;
If we follow the blue line up we see that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:32.960
was originally known as the Kingdom of the&nbsp;
West Franks and that, at the very top, West&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:39.680
Francia connects the Empire of Charlemagne. Now,&nbsp;
also connecting to Charlemagne is East Francia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:45.680
which corresponds to Germany. It eventually&nbsp;
became the Holy Roman Empire but take note&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:52.800
that the core part of the Holy Roman Empire was&nbsp;
always what historians refer to as the medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:59.520
Kingdom of Germany. So, although Germany is&nbsp;
usually said to have been founded in 1871,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:04.080
I think it’s fair to say that&nbsp;
Germany is actually much, much older.

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:09.920
Now, going back up to the top, I want to make the&nbsp;
point that, originally, France and Germany were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:15.760
a single country: Francia. And Francia actually&nbsp;
goes back much farther than Charlemagne. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:21.680
it goes all the way back to 481 (which is&nbsp;
just 5 years after the fall of Rome). So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:29.920
I’ve got a choice here. Do I use the year 481 for&nbsp;
both France and Germany or do I use the year 843,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:35.680
which is when the Treaty of Verdun split the&nbsp;
Franks into three pieces (with only two pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:43.440
surviving: West and East, which became France&nbsp;
and Germany). Well, I’m going to go with 843&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:49.040
because that’s when each side started to develop&nbsp;
their own unique histories. Now, as you can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:54.960
this puts France and Germany into a three-way&nbsp;
tie with Scotland so, like I mentioned earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:59.680
I think we need to bring up the issue of&nbsp;
historicity here. When it comes to Scotland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:06.320
there is some debate over whether 843 actually&nbsp;
marks the point in which Scotland became truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:13.520
united. Some argue that the reign of Constantine&nbsp;
II, starting in 900, would be more accurate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:19.520
In contrast, 843 is a very solid date for&nbsp;
when Charlemagne’s Empire was split. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:25.440
while I’m going to keep Scotland at 843, I’m&nbsp;
going to put France and Germany above it.

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:32.160
Okay, next up, I want to address Hungary. As you&nbsp;
can see, Hungary was founded in the year 1000.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:37.680
Eventually, it was inherited by the Habsburgs&nbsp;
(who were from Austria and had become Holy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:43.440
Roman Emperors). But because they held Austria&nbsp;
and Hungary separately from the imperial title,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:49.520
when the Holy Roman Empire fell apart, they&nbsp;
continued to rule both Austria and Hungary (with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:54.560
Germany going in a totally different direction&nbsp;
at this point). Now, another part of the Holy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:59.120
Roman Empire that eventually developed&nbsp;
its own unique history is Switzerland.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:06.320
It actually traces its roots all the way back to&nbsp;
1291 so that’s the date I’m going to use for it.

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:12.320
We now come to the Slavic countries. The only two&nbsp;
West Slavic countries that go back to medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:18.320
times are Czechia and Poland. For most of&nbsp;
its history, Czechia was known as Bohemia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:22.320
and while it was held in personal&nbsp;
union with Hungary for a long time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:27.520
and before that was a part of the Holy Roman&nbsp;
Empire, it actually has independent roots that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:34.800
go all the way back to 870. Poland was founded&nbsp;
a bit later, in 966. It later went through a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:39.520
period of fragmentation only to re-emerge&nbsp;
during the 14th century, eventually forming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:46.400
a union with Lithuania. Lithuania was founded&nbsp;
in 1253 so let’s add that to the list as well.

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:51.040
Now, when it comes to East Slavic&nbsp;
countries, there are three: Russia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:59.040
Belarus, and Ukraine. They all trace their origin&nbsp;
back to Kyivan Rus, which was founded in 882. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:05.840
should I give 882 to all three countries?&nbsp;
Well, since I went with when Francia split&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:12.240
for the start dates for both France and Germany,&nbsp;
I think I should go with when Kyivan Rus split&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:19.920
for the start dates for Russia, Belarus, and&nbsp;
Ukraine. This occurred around the year 1200.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:26.960
Galicia-Volhynia roughly corresponds to Ukraine&nbsp;
and while it was eventually conquered by Poland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:32.480
and then later Russia, the region did develop&nbsp;
its own unique language and culture. Likewise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:38.560
it was the Grand-Principality of Vladimir, which&nbsp;
eventually evolved into Moscow, which eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:43.920
evolved into Russia. And, while it’s not shown&nbsp;
on the chart, there also existed a medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:50.560
principality called Polotsk, which followed&nbsp;
a different trajectory and is today Belurus.

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:56.640
In terms of South Slavic countries,&nbsp;
the oldest is Croatia, founded in 925.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:02.080
It entered into a personal union with Hungary,&nbsp;
around the same time that Serbia emerged as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:07.440
independent entity (which was in 1091).&nbsp;
It’s not on the chart but there was also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:14.560
an independent state called Duklja around this&nbsp;
time (starting from 1077) which corresponds to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:19.360
modern day Montenegro. Finally, came&nbsp;
Bosnia, which formally split from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:26.400
Hungary in 1377. During that same century, two&nbsp;
non-slavic countries also split from Hungary:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:34.160
Wallachia in 1330 (which eventually became a&nbsp;
core part of Romania) and Moldova in 1359. Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:41.440
and I should also point out Arbanon, which emerged&nbsp;
in 1190 and is the earliest Albanian state.

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:47.040
Alright, we now have only 1 country&nbsp;
left: Bulgaria. And it’s good that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:53.040
this one came last because, although it’s&nbsp;
shown on this chart as beginning in 864,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:59.840
it actually goes back (as an independent&nbsp;
entity) all the way to 681. Which means that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:06.400
according my criteria, Bulgaria&nbsp;
is the oldest European country;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:13.440
followed by France and Germany (tied at 2),&nbsp;
Scotland at 4, and Czechia (formerly known as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:22.560
Bohemia) at 5. Rounding out the top 10 would be&nbsp;
Norway, England, Croatia, Iceland, and Denmark.

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:26.960
Now, like I said, there are multiple ways to&nbsp;
answer the question and I’m sure many of you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:33.520
have strong opinions about my choices. So let me&nbsp;
give you some alternative answers. Bulgaria was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:38.480
ruled by the Byzantines and Ottomans for much&nbsp;
of its history, so if you want to go with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:46.640
oldest continuously-existing independent European&nbsp;
country, that would be France (because there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.640 --> 00:13:53.040
a space in-between the Holy Roman Empire and&nbsp;
the German Empire when Germany was not united).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:59.920
However, if you want to include microstates,&nbsp;
the oldest European country would be San Marino,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:08.000
which was founded in 301 CE. But, if you also&nbsp;
wanted to include the transcontinental countries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:16.000
well then you’re looking at Armenia (which was&nbsp;
founded all the way back in 331 BCE). Finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:20.000
if you want to throw out all of the rules and&nbsp;
just go with the country where the earliest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:25.600
European civilization was established, well&nbsp;
that would probably be Greece because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:31.920
Minoan civilization on the island of&nbsp;
Crete goes back to around 3000 BCE.

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:39.040
So, yeah, like I said at the beginning, there&nbsp;
are lots of ways to answer the question. My&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:44.000
preference is to go with either Bulgaria (if&nbsp;
you’re okay with periods of foreign rule)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:49.840
or France (if you’re not). Let me know in the&nbsp;
comments what your opinion is. And, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:55.440
if you want to get a copy of the chart as a&nbsp;
poster, just head over to UsefulCharts.com&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.440 --> 00:15:00.640
or just use the link in the description&nbsp;
or pinned comment. I’ll also leave a link&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:06.400
on screen to a video we did previously that&nbsp;
goes through the chart in even greater detail.

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:24.400
Until next time, thanks for watching.

